<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-number-systems">

  <title>Number Systems</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <em>Prerequisites.</em>
      Familiarity with mathematical notation from
      <xref ref="sec-mathematical-language-notation"/>
      and arithmetic from
      <xref ref="sec-arithmetic-foundations"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>
    </introduction>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          distinguish natural, whole, integer, rational, irrational,
          real, and complex numbers;
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          classify numbers using the smallest standard number system
          that contains them;
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>interpret terminating and repeating decimals;</p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>convert repeating decimals to fractions;</p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>interpret absolute value as distance;</p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>use interval notation;</p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>work with complex numbers in standard form;</p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          compute complex sums, products, quotients, conjugates,
          and moduli;
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          understand the containment relationships among the standard
          number systems;
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          recognize closure properties and the reason number systems
          are extended.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- WHY NUMBER SYSTEMS EXPAND -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-why-number-systems-expand">

    <title>Why Number Systems Expand</title>

    <p>
      The history and structure of mathematics repeatedly show the same
      pattern: an existing number system is sufficient for some operations
      but not for others.
    </p>

    <p>The natural numbers are sufficient for counting:</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        1,2,3,\ldots
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>but subtraction may leave the natural numbers:</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        3-5=-2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>The integers include negative values, but division may leave the integers:</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        1\div2=\frac12.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The rational numbers include fractions, but equations such as
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x^2=2
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      require numbers that cannot be represented as ratios of integers.
    </p>

    <p>
      The real numbers include those irrational values, but the equation
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x^2=-1
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>has no real solution.</p>

    <p>
      This motivates a sequence of increasingly large number systems.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- NATURAL NUMBERS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-natural-numbers">

    <title>The Natural Numbers</title>

    <p>The symbol</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \N
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is the <em>blackboard-bold capital N</em> and denotes the natural
      numbers.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <title>Natural Numbers</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          In this text, the <em>natural numbers</em> are the positive
          counting numbers:
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \N=\{1,2,3,4,\ldots\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The ellipsis indicates that the counting pattern continues
      indefinitely.
    </p>

    <warning>
      <title>Convention for the Natural Numbers</title>

      <p>
        Some authors include <m>0</m> in the natural numbers.
      </p>

      <p>In this text,</p>

      <p>
        <md>
          \N=\{1,2,3,\ldots\},
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        so <m>0\notin\N</m> unless another convention is explicitly stated.
      </p>
    </warning>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- WHOLE NUMBERS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-whole-numbers">

    <title>The Whole Numbers</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Whole Numbers</title>

      <statement>
        <p>The <em>whole numbers</em> are</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \{0,1,2,3,\ldots\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      This terminology is common in elementary mathematics.
    </p>

    <p>
      In more advanced mathematics, the natural numbers and integers are
      usually more important as formal number systems, so a special standard
      symbol for the whole numbers is used less consistently.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- INTEGERS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-integers">

    <title>The Integers</title>

    <p>The symbol</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \Z
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is the <em>blackboard-bold capital Z</em> and denotes the integers.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <title>Integers</title>

      <statement>
        <p>The <em>integers</em> are</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \Z=\{\ldots,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,\ldots\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The letter <m>\Z</m> is traditionally associated with the German word
      <em>Zahlen</em>, meaning numbers.
    </p>

    <p>
      The integers extend the natural numbers by including zero and the
      negative whole numbers.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- OPPOSITES -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-opposites">

    <title>Opposites</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Opposite</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Every integer <m>a</m> has an <em>opposite</em>, or
          <em>additive inverse</em>, written
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            -a.
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>The two numbers satisfy</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            a+(-a)=0.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        8+(-8)=0.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Opposites lie the same distance from zero on the real number line but
      on opposite sides.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- RATIONAL NUMBERS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rational-numbers">

    <title>The Rational Numbers</title>

    <p>The symbol</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \Q
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is the <em>blackboard-bold capital Q</em> and denotes the rational
      numbers.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <title>Rational Number</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A number is <em>rational</em> if it can be written in the form
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \frac{a}{b},
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>where</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            a,b\in\Z,\qquad b\neq0.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Thus,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \Q=
        \left\{
        \frac{a}{b}:a,b\in\Z,\ b\neq0
        \right\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The letter <m>Q</m> may be remembered through the word
      <em>quotient</em>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rational-examples">

    <title>Examples of Rational Numbers</title>

    <p>Every integer is rational because</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        n=\frac{n}{1}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Therefore,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        5=\frac51,
        \qquad
        -12=\frac{-12}{1}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Other examples include</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \frac23,
        \qquad
        -\frac74,
        \qquad
        \frac{19}{8}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Zero is rational because</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        0=\frac01.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- DECIMAL REPRESENTATIONS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rational-decimals">

    <title>Decimal Representations of Rational Numbers</title>

    <p>
      Every rational number has a decimal expansion that either terminates
      or eventually repeats.
    </p>

    <p>Examples of terminating decimals include</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \frac12=0.5,
        \qquad
        \frac18=0.125.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Examples of repeating decimals include</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \frac13=0.333\ldots
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>and</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \frac{2}{11}=0.181818\ldots.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-repeating-decimal-notation">

    <title>Repeating Decimal Notation</title>

    <p>A bar may be placed over repeating digits.</p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        0.\overline3
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>means</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        0.3333\ldots.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The horizontal bar is called an <em>overline</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this context, the expression is read
      <q>zero point three repeating.</q>
    </p>

    <p>Similarly,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        0.\overline{27}=0.27272727\ldots.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-repeating-decimal-fraction">

    <title>Converting a Repeating Decimal to a Fraction</title>

    <p>
      Repeating decimals can be converted to fractions algebraically.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Convert a Repeating Decimal to a Fraction</title>

      <statement>

        <p>Convert</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            0.\overline3
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>to a fraction.</p>

        <table>
          <title>Conversion Steps</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Let the decimal equal <m>x</m>.</cell>
              <cell><m>x=0.3333\ldots</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Multiply by <m>10</m>.</cell>
              <cell><m>10x=3.3333\ldots</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Subtract the original equation.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>10x-x=3.3333\ldots-0.3333\ldots</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell><m>9x=3</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Divide by <m>9</m>.</cell>
              <cell><m>x=\frac13</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>
            0.\overline3=\frac13
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-longer-repeating-blocks">

    <title>Longer Repeating Blocks</title>

    <p>
      If several digits repeat, multiply by an appropriate power of
      <m>10</m>.
    </p>

    <p>For example, let</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x=0.\overline{27}=0.272727\ldots.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Because two digits repeat, multiply by <m>100</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        100x=27.272727\ldots.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Subtracting the original equation gives</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        99x=27.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Therefore,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x=\frac{27}{99}=\frac3{11}.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- IRRATIONAL NUMBERS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-irrational-numbers">

    <title>Irrational Numbers</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Irrational Number</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          An <em>irrational number</em> is a real number that cannot be
          written as a ratio of two integers.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Examples include</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \sqrt2,
        \qquad
        \sqrt3,
        \qquad
        \pi,
        \qquad
        e.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Their decimal expansions do not terminate and do not eventually
      repeat.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rational-vs-irrational">

    <title>Rational versus Irrational</title>

    <p>
      Every real number is either rational or irrational.
    </p>

    <p>No real number can be both.</p>

    <p>Thus, within the real numbers,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \text{rational}
        \qquad\text{and}\qquad
        \text{irrational}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>form mutually exclusive categories.</p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sqrt2-irrational">

    <title>Why the Square Root of Two Is Irrational</title>

    <p>
      The number <m>\sqrt2</m> is one of the classic examples of an
      irrational number.
    </p>

    <p>A complete proof uses contradiction.</p>

    <p>Suppose</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \sqrt2=\frac ab
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      for integers <m>a,b</m> in lowest terms.
    </p>

    <p>Squaring gives</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        2=\frac{a^2}{b^2},
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>so</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a^2=2b^2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus <m>a^2</m> is even, which forces <m>a</m> to be even.
    </p>

    <p>Write</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a=2k.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Then</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        4k^2=2b^2,
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>so</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        b^2=2k^2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Therefore <m>b</m> is also even.</p>

    <p>
      But then <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> share a factor of <m>2</m>,
      contradicting the assumption that <m>a/b</m> was in lowest terms.
    </p>

    <p>Hence,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \sqrt2\notin\Q.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The proof method used here is developed formally in
      <xref ref="sec-methods-of-proof"/>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- REAL NUMBERS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-real-numbers">

    <title>The Real Numbers</title>

    <p>The symbol</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \R
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is the <em>blackboard-bold capital R</em> and denotes the real
      numbers.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <title>Real Numbers</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          The <em>real numbers</em> consist of all rational and irrational
          numbers.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The real numbers correspond to the points on the real number line.
    </p>

    <p>Thus numbers such as</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        -4,
        \qquad
        0,
        \qquad
        \frac35,
        \qquad
        \sqrt2,
        \qquad
        \pi
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>are all real.</p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-real-number-line">

    <title>The Real Number Line</title>

    <p>
      The real number line gives a geometric interpretation of
      <m>\R</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Numbers increase as one moves to the right and decrease as one moves
      to the left.
    </p>

    <p>If</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a&lt;b,
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      then <m>a</m> lies to the left of <m>b</m>.
    </p>

    <p>If</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a&gt;b,
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      then <m>a</m> lies to the right of <m>b</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- ABSOLUTE VALUE -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-absolute-value-distance">

    <title>Absolute Value as Distance</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Absolute Value</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          The <em>absolute value</em> of a real number <m>x</m>, written
          <m>|x|</m>, is its distance from zero on the real number line.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        |5|=5,
        \qquad
        |-5|=5.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Absolute value is always nonnegative:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        |x|\geq0.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-distance-between-real-numbers">

    <title>Distance Between Real Numbers</title>

    <p>
      The distance between two real numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> is
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        |a-b|.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Equivalently,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        |a-b|=|b-a|.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus the order of the two points does not affect their distance.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-triangle-inequality">

    <title>The Triangle Inequality</title>

    <p>
      Absolute value satisfies one of the most important inequalities in
      mathematics.
    </p>

    <theorem>
      <title>Triangle Inequality</title>

      <statement>
        <p>For all real numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>,</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            |a+b|\leq|a|+|b|.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      Geometrically, the direct distance between two points cannot exceed
      the distance obtained by traveling through an intermediate point.
    </p>

    <p>
      This inequality later becomes fundamental in analysis, vector spaces,
      metric spaces, and functional analysis.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- INTERVAL NOTATION -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-interval-notation">

    <title>Interval Notation</title>

    <p>
      Interval notation provides a compact way to describe sets of real
      numbers.
    </p>

    <p>The open interval</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (a,b)
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>represents</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \{x\in\R:a&lt;x&lt;b\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Parentheses indicate that the endpoints are not included.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-closed-intervals">

    <title>Closed Intervals</title>

    <p>The closed interval</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        [a,b]
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>represents</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \{x\in\R:a\leq x\leq b\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Square brackets indicate that the endpoints are included.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-half-open-intervals">

    <title>Half-Open Intervals</title>

    <p>
      An interval may include one endpoint but exclude the other.
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        [a,b)
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>means</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a\leq x&lt;b.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Similarly,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (a,b]
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>means</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a&lt;x\leq b.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- INFINITY -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-infinity">

    <title>Infinity</title>

    <p>The symbol</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \infty
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is called the <em>infinity symbol</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In ordinary real-number arithmetic, infinity is not itself a real
      number.
    </p>

    <p>
      Rather, it is used to describe quantities or sets that are unbounded.
    </p>

    <p>
      Likewise, <m>-\infty</m> describes unbounded behavior in the negative
      direction.
    </p>

    <warning>
      <title>Infinity Is Not an Ordinary Real Number</title>

      <p>
        Expressions involving infinity must be interpreted according to
        context.
      </p>

      <p>
        One should not automatically manipulate <m>\infty</m> as though it
        were an ordinary element of <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
    </warning>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-unbounded-intervals">

    <title>Unbounded Intervals</title>

    <p>The interval</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        [2,\infty)
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>represents</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \{x\in\R:x\geq2\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Similarly,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (-\infty,5)
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>represents</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \{x\in\R:x&lt;5\}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Parentheses are always used next to <m>\infty</m> and
      <m>-\infty</m> because infinity is not an endpoint contained in
      <m>\R</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- COMPLEX NUMBERS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-need-for-complex-numbers">

    <title>The Need for Complex Numbers</title>

    <p>Consider the equation</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x^2=-1.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      No real number satisfies this equation because the square of every
      nonzero real number is positive, while
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        0^2=0.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      To solve such equations, the real number system is extended to the
      complex numbers.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-imaginary-unit">

    <title>The Imaginary Unit</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Imaginary Unit</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          The <em>imaginary unit</em>, written <m>i</m>, is defined by
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            i^2=-1.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The symbol <m>i</m> therefore allows expressions involving square
      roots of negative real numbers to be represented within a larger
      number system.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-numbers">

    <title>The Complex Numbers</title>

    <p>The symbol</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \C
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is the <em>blackboard-bold capital C</em> and denotes the complex
      numbers.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <title>Complex Number</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>complex number</em> is a number of the form
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            a+bi,
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          where <m>a,b\in\R</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Thus,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \C=\{a+bi:a,b\in\R\}.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-real-imaginary-parts">

    <title>Real and Imaginary Parts</title>

    <p>If</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        z=a+bi,
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      then <m>a</m> is called the <em>real part</em> of <m>z</m> and
      <m>b</m> is called the <em>imaginary part</em>.
    </p>

    <p>These are written</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \operatorname{Re}(z)=a,
        \qquad
        \operatorname{Im}(z)=b.
      </md>
    </p>

    <warning>
      <title>The Imaginary Part</title>

      <p>
        By standard convention, the imaginary part of <m>a+bi</m> is
        <m>b</m>, not <m>bi</m>.
      </p>
    </warning>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-real-as-complex">

    <title>Real Numbers as Complex Numbers</title>

    <p>
      Every real number can be viewed as a complex number with imaginary
      part zero:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a=a+0i.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Therefore,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \R\subset\C.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      A number having real part zero is called <em>purely imaginary</em> and
      has the form
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        bi.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-equality-complex">

    <title>Equality of Complex Numbers</title>

    <p>
      Two complex numbers are equal exactly when their corresponding real
      and imaginary parts are equal.
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a+bi=c+di
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>if and only if</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a=c
        \qquad\text{and}\qquad
        b=d.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- COMPLEX ARITHMETIC -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-addition">

    <title>Complex Addition</title>

    <p>
      Complex numbers are added by combining corresponding real and
      imaginary parts:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (a+bi)+(c+di)
        =
        (a+c)+(b+d)i.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (3+4i)+(2-7i)=5-3i.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-subtraction">

    <title>Complex Subtraction</title>

    <p>
      Similarly,
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (a+bi)-(c+di)
        =
        (a-c)+(b-d)i.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (5+2i)-(3-4i)=2+6i.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-powers-of-i">

    <title>Powers of the Imaginary Unit</title>

    <p>The first four positive powers of <m>i</m> are</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        i^1=i,
        \qquad
        i^2=-1,
        \qquad
        i^3=-i,
        \qquad
        i^4=1.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      These values repeat in a cycle of length four.
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        i^5=i,
        \qquad
        i^6=-1,
        \qquad
        i^7=-i,
        \qquad
        i^8=1.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore large powers of <m>i</m> can be simplified by determining
      the exponent modulo <m>4</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-multiplication">

    <title>Complex Multiplication</title>

    <p>
      Complex multiplication follows the distributive property together
      with
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        i^2=-1.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Thus,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (a+bi)(c+di)
        =
        ac+adi+bci+bdi^2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Since <m>i^2=-1</m>,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (a+bi)(c+di)
        =
        (ac-bd)+(ad+bc)i.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-complex-multiplication">

    <title>Worked Example: Complex Multiplication</title>

    <example>
      <title>Multiply Complex Numbers</title>

      <statement>

        <p>Compute</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            (2+3i)(4-i).
          </md>
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Complex Multiplication Steps</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>8-2i+12i-3i^2</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Use <m>i^2=-1</m>.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>8+10i+3</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Combine real terms.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>11+10i</m>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>
            11+10i
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- COMPLEX CONJUGATES -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-conjugates">

    <title>Complex Conjugates</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Complex Conjugate</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          If
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            z=a+bi,
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          then the <em>complex conjugate</em> of <m>z</m> is
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \overline z=a-bi.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>For example, if</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        z=3+5i,
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>then</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \overline z=3-5i.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-product-with-conjugate">

    <title>Product with a Conjugate</title>

    <p>
      Multiplying a complex number by its conjugate produces a nonnegative
      real number:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (a+bi)(a-bi)
        =
        a^2+b^2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This follows because the imaginary terms cancel:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a^2-abi+abi-b^2i^2
        =
        a^2+b^2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This property is especially useful when dividing complex numbers.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-division">

    <title>Complex Division</title>

    <p>
      To divide by a complex number, multiply the numerator and denominator
      by the conjugate of the denominator.
    </p>

    <p>For</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \frac{a+bi}{c+di},
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      with <m>c+di\neq0</m>, multiply by
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \frac{c-di}{c-di}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The denominator becomes
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        c^2+d^2,
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      which is real.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-complex-division">

    <title>Worked Example: Complex Division</title>

    <example>
      <title>Divide Complex Numbers</title>

      <statement>

        <p>Simplify</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \frac{2+i}{3-i}.
          </md>
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Complex Division Steps</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply by the conjugate.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>
                  \frac{2+i}{3-i}
                  \frac{3+i}{3+i}
                </m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Expand the numerator.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>
                  (2+i)(3+i)=5+5i
                </m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Expand the denominator.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>
                  (3-i)(3+i)=10
                </m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>
                  \frac12+\frac12i
                </m>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>
            \frac12+\frac12i
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- COMPLEX PLANE -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-plane">

    <title>The Complex Plane</title>

    <p>
      A complex number
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        z=a+bi
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      can be represented geometrically by the point
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        (a,b).
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The horizontal axis represents the real part and the vertical axis
      represents the imaginary part.
    </p>

    <p>
      This coordinate system is called the <em>complex plane</em> or
      <em>Argand plane</em>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-modulus">

    <title>Complex Modulus</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Complex Modulus</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          For
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            z=a+bi,
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          the <em>modulus</em> of <m>z</m> is
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            |z|=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Geometrically, <m>|z|</m> is the distance from the origin to the point
      <m>(a,b)</m> in the complex plane.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same vertical-bar notation used for real absolute value is used
      for complex modulus.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-conjugate-modulus">

    <title>Worked Example: Conjugate and Modulus</title>

    <example>
      <title>Find a Conjugate and Modulus</title>

      <statement>

        <p>Let</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            z=-3+4i.
          </md>
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Conjugate and Modulus Steps</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Change the sign of the imaginary part.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\overline z=-3-4i</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Use the modulus formula.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>|z|=\sqrt{(-3)^2+4^2}</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>|z|=\sqrt{25}=5</m>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>
            \overline z=-3-4i,
            \qquad
            |z|=5
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- CONTAINMENT -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-number-system-containment">

    <title>Containment of the Standard Number Systems</title>

    <p>The standard number systems satisfy</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \N\subset\Z\subset\Q\subset\R\subset\C.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>The symbol</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \subset
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      is called a <em>subset symbol</em> and is read
      <q>is a subset of.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus every natural number is an integer, every integer is rational,
      every rational number is real, and every real number is complex.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-classifying-numbers">

    <title>Classifying Numbers</title>

    <p>
      A number may belong to several number systems simultaneously.
    </p>

    <p>For example,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        5\in\N.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Because of the containment chain,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        5\in\Z,
        \qquad
        5\in\Q,
        \qquad
        5\in\R,
        \qquad
        5\in\C.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      When asked to classify a number, it is often useful to identify the
      <em>smallest standard number system</em> containing it.
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>5</m>, that system is <m>\N</m>.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-classification">

    <title>Worked Example: Classification</title>

    <example>
      <title>Classify a Rational Number</title>

      <statement>

        <p>Determine which standard number systems contain</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            -\frac74.
          </md>
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Classification Steps</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Natural number?</cell>
              <cell>No</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Integer?</cell>
              <cell>No</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Ratio of integers?</cell>
              <cell>Yes, <m>-7/4</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Therefore rational?</cell>
              <cell>Yes</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Every rational number is real.</cell>
              <cell><m>-\frac74\in\R</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Every real number is complex.</cell>
              <cell><m>-\frac74\in\C</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>
            -\frac74\in\Q\subset\R\subset\C
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- CLOSURE -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-number-system-closure">

    <title>Closure</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Closure</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A set is <em>closed under an operation</em> if performing that
          operation on elements of the set always produces another element
          of the same set.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      For example, <m>\Z</m> is closed under addition because
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        a,b\in\Z
        \Longrightarrow
        a+b\in\Z.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The integers are also closed under subtraction and multiplication.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, they are not closed under division because
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        1,2\in\Z
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>but</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \frac12\notin\Z.
      </md>
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-closure-table">

    <title>Closure of Familiar Number Systems</title>

    <p>
      Ignoring operations that are undefined, such as division by zero, a
      useful summary is:
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Closure of Familiar Number Systems</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>System</cell>
          <cell>Addition</cell>
          <cell>Subtraction</cell>
          <cell>Multiplication</cell>
          <cell>Division</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\N</m></cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\Z</m></cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\Q</m></cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\R</m></cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><m>\C</m></cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      For division, <q>yes</q> means division by a nonzero element remains
      in the system.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- EQUATIONS AND EXTENSIONS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-number-systems-equations">

    <title>Number Systems and Solving Equations</title>

    <p>
      The solution to an equation may depend on the number system being
      used.
    </p>

    <p>Consider</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x+5=2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This has no solution in <m>\N</m>, but it has the integer solution
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x=-3.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Consider</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        2x=1.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This has no integer solution, but it has the rational solution
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x=\frac12.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Consider</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x^2=2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This has no rational solution, but it has real solutions
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x=\pm\sqrt2.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>Finally,</p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x^2=-1
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      has no real solution, but it has complex solutions
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        x=\pm i.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This progression illustrates why larger number systems are needed.
    </p>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-number-system-misconceptions">

    <title>Common Misconceptions</title>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Every Decimal Is Irrational</title>

      <p>False.</p>

      <p>
        Terminating and repeating decimals are rational.
      </p>

      <p>For example,</p>

      <p>
        <md>
          0.125=\frac18.
        </md>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Every Square Root Is Irrational</title>

      <p>False.</p>

      <p>For example,</p>

      <p>
        <md>
          \sqrt9=3\in\N.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Only some square roots are irrational.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Infinity Is a Real Number</title>

      <p>
        The symbol <m>\infty</m> is not an ordinary element of
        <m>\R</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        It represents unbounded behavior in contexts such as intervals and
        limits.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>The Imaginary Part of a Complex Number</title>

      <p>
        By standard convention,
      </p>

      <p>
        <md>
          \operatorname{Im}(a+bi)=b,
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        not <m>bi</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
      <title>Complex Numbers Are Not Real in the Everyday Sense</title>

      <p>
        The terms <em>real</em> and <em>complex</em> are technical
        mathematical names.
      </p>

      <p>
        Complex numbers are perfectly legitimate mathematical objects.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- WORKED EXAMPLES -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-number-systems-worked-examples">

    <title>Worked Examples</title>

    <example>
      <title>Convert a Repeating Decimal</title>

      <statement>

        <p>Convert</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            0.\overline{18}
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>to a fraction.</p>

        <table>
          <title>Conversion Steps</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Let the decimal equal <m>x</m>.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>x=0.181818\ldots</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                Two digits repeat, so multiply by <m>100</m>.
              </cell>
              <cell>
                <m>100x=18.181818\ldots</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Subtract.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>99x=18</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Solve.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>x=\frac{18}{99}=\frac2{11}</m>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>
            0.\overline{18}=\frac2{11}
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>


    <example>
      <title>Distance on the Real Line</title>

      <statement>

        <p>
          Find the distance between <m>-6</m> and <m>4</m>.
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Distance Steps</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the distance formula.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>|-6-4|</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Simplify inside the absolute value.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>|-10|</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Evaluate.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>10</m>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <md>
            10
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>


    <example>
      <title>Classify a Number</title>

      <statement>

        <p>Classify</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \sqrt{49}.
          </md>
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Classification Steps</title>

          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Evaluate the square root.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>\sqrt{49}=7</m>
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Find the smallest standard number system.</cell>
              <cell>
                <m>7\in\N</m>
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

      </statement>

      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>\sqrt{49}</m> is natural, integer, rational, real, and complex.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- EXERCISES -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-number-systems-exercises">

    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Classify each number using the smallest standard number system that
          contains it:
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            8,
            \qquad
            -6,
            \qquad
            \frac35,
            \qquad
            \sqrt7,
            \qquad
            2+3i.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether each decimal is rational or irrational:
        </p>

        <p>
          <md>
            0.25,
            \qquad
            0.\overline7,
            \qquad
            0.101001000100001\ldots.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Convert</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            0.\overline6
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>to a fraction.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Convert</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            0.\overline{45}
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>to a fraction.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Write</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            -3&lt;x\leq5
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>using interval notation.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Write</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            [2,\infty)
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>as an inequality.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the distance between <m>-7</m> and <m>9</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Simplify:</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            i^{10}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute:</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            (4+3i)+(2-8i).
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute:</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            (5+2i)(3-i).
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Simplify:</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            \frac{2+i}{3-i}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the conjugate of</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            z=6-5i.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the modulus of</p>

        <p>
          <md>
            z=5+12i.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why <m>\Z</m> is not closed under division.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give an example showing that <m>\N</m> is not closed under
          subtraction.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why every integer is rational.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why every real number may also be viewed as a complex
          number.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- CONNECTIONS -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-number-systems-connections">

    <title>Section Connections</title>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Chapter Connection</title>

      <p>
        Number systems provide the underlying collections on which most
        later mathematical structures are built. Sets are developed formally
        in <xref ref="sec-elementary-set-theory"/>; algebra studies arithmetic
        structures over systems such as
        <m>\Z</m>, <m>\Q</m>, <m>\R</m>, and <m>\C</m>; real analysis
        studies the deeper properties of <m>\R</m>; complex analysis studies
        functions on <m>\C</m>; and number theory focuses strongly on the
        structure of integers and related systems.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

  </subsection>

</section>
